WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.403
-We're back with Hilary Duff.
This is very exciting.

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:05.137
-Below 60.
-Yeah.

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:06.639
-What would you
describe these as?

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.041
In-home fragrances?
-Yes I would.

00:00:09.041 --> 00:00:10.977
I would say
they're in-home fragrances.

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.478
They're plug-ins.
-They're plug-ins.

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:15.382
-You know, we all kind of grew
up with said plug-in.

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:18.151
-Yes.
-They are updated, my friend.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:20.319
-And you are the chief
brand director

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:21.654
for these fragrances.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.422
-Which basically means
I get to be really bossy with

00:00:23.422 --> 00:00:25.391
all the boys
that started the company.

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:26.692
-Oh, that's very nice.
-Yeah.

00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:28.962
And I get to tell them
like, "I don't like that.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.964
No, that sticks out
too far from the wall.

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.899
Change this, change that.
Oh, what about this scent?

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.467
Oh, that scent.
I like this thing together."

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.602
-And you seem like a very
fun person to work with.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:38.638
[ Laughter ]
-It's a lot of texts.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.272
It's a lot of rapid-fire texts.
-Oh, okay.

00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:41.774
So it's all on texts.
-No, no, no.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:43.109
We have meetings,
too, of course.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.643
But, no, they're great.

00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:46.445
-So, a lot of it
is how the product looks,

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:47.880
but a lot of it
must be the scents

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.515
and then also
the names of the scents.

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:50.917
First of all,
before I get to the names,

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:52.351
do you feel like you have
a good nose for this?

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.186
Have you been told over
the years that you do?

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:55.421
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:00:55.421 --> 00:00:57.556
-I mean, by the way,
I'm always complimented by

00:00:57.556 --> 00:00:59.992
like, the way
that I smell, so...

00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:02.261
-Like, not by the way
you smell other things,

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:03.363
but the way
you actually smell.

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:04.898
-I smell other things
pretty well, too,

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.231
but that's because
I've been obsessed with

00:01:06.231 --> 00:01:07.834
perfume and scents
for a long time.

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:09.268
-That's very --
So, you've been a --

00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:11.070
I know those kind of people
that they walk in,

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.072
and everybody's like,
"Wow, you smell great."

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.174
That's happened to you
your whole life?

00:01:15.174 --> 00:01:17.110
-Since I was a toddler, yeah.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.744
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:21.781
No, but since I started
buying perfume, I guess,

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:24.383
ever since the Cucumber Melon
from Bath &amp; Body Works, guys.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:25.651
-I was...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:26.919
-People know.

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:28.654
But that -- Well, I'm glad
you made the distinction,

00:01:28.654 --> 00:01:29.855
because you're not saying,

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:31.890
"Look, there's no one
could ever smell like me.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.159
It comes from within."

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:35.427
You're basically saying
it is product-based.

00:01:35.427 --> 00:01:37.763
-It's both, Seth. It's both.
-It's both.

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:39.565
You've got to have
a base coat of just being,

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:40.867
like, a good-smelling person.

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.168
-Yes. Yes.
-Yeah.

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:43.570
-I'm sweating.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.038 --> 00:01:47.973
-And by the way, the more --
Can I just say?

00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:49.075
The more you're smelling.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:51.377
Yeah, it's, like --
It's outstanding.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.079
[ Laughter ]

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:54.413
Alright, so, the names.

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:57.850
Are you coming up with
these names for these scents?

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.954
-Yeah. So, the product
is very, like, universal.

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:05.457
It can look great in any home.
It's really modern and updated.

00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:06.993
And I was like,
"We got to give the --"

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.828
I mean, I'm the shopper
in my household.

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.130
I'm sure Alexi is the same.
-100%.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.666
-She's the consumer.
We're buying for the house.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:16.202
We're the ones
that get ad-targeted.

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:19.806
-Yeah.
-They get me every time.

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.440
And I was like,
"We got to hook people in.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.676
Like, what is --
Let's give this a story."

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.178
Let's give these names like --
Let's be very playful with it."

00:02:26.178 --> 00:02:29.549
Also, the scents last a month,
so, you know, when you're done

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:33.485
with it, you, like, refill
a cartridge with something else.

00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:35.622
And it's just, like,
constantly evolving,

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:36.856
which is kind of my personality.

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:38.023
I'm always wanting
to try something new

00:02:38.023 --> 00:02:39.091
and do something new.

00:02:39.091 --> 00:02:40.159
So, yeah,
the names are very fun.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.593
-I'm gonna read you some.
-Okay.

00:02:41.593 --> 00:02:43.563
-If Citrus Were a Feeling.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:45.698
-That's a very unique name
for a smell.

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.867
-Okay, are you judging
or do you like it?

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.235
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.170
-I want you to take
"unique" home with you

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.339
and decide
what you think it means.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.341
No, I like it,
but I like the better one.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:57.243
I like the second one more.
-Okay.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:59.245
-No, I'm going to put
my favorite one third.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.913
Then Mint Disco on Ice.
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:03.415
-I like Mint Disco on Ice.
-Okay. You know what?

00:03:03.415 --> 00:03:05.384
The men love that one.
-Do they really?

00:03:05.384 --> 00:03:07.152
-Yeah. I don't know why men
love, like, mint

00:03:07.152 --> 00:03:09.054
and, like, fresh
and, like, cooling and pine

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:10.256
and things like that.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.490
-Well, now I feel
like you're judging.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.826
-Well, I'm a little
judgmental of that one.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:16.262
I think it's a little more
geared towards men, but...

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:19.065
-Gotcha. Alright.
-I love it. I still love it.

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:21.301
-Oh, nobody believes that.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.005
-Okay, Seth.
-But here's the winner.

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.641
Vanilla Buys a Timeshare
in Paradise.

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.109
I mean, come on.
That is...

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:32.978
-Don't you want to buy it?
-That I want to buy.

00:03:32.978 --> 00:03:35.047
And I don't even --
Like, vanilla would not be --

00:03:35.047 --> 00:03:36.616
is not, like,
a top-three scent of mine,

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:39.185
but I have so much appreciation
for how that --

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:41.520
Now I'm using it in
the best way I possibly can.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.155
That is incredibly unique.

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:45.224
A.I. would never come up with

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:46.959
Vanilla Buys
a Timeshare in Paradise.

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.327
-Thank you! I agree.

00:03:48.327 --> 00:03:50.196
Some things
still need that human touch.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:51.864
-And, so, when --
And do these just sort of --

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.199
I mean, I'm sure
it's a whole team,

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:54.733
but do you, like --
Do you, like, smell it

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:56.335
and then go into,
like, a fugue state

00:03:56.335 --> 00:03:58.504
and then you go, "Vanilla
Buys a Timeshare in Paradise"?

00:03:58.504 --> 00:03:59.939
-It just --
Yeah, it just comes to me.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.141
I start, like, shaking
and I'm like...

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:05.011
-You just, like, lie in
a milk bath until --

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.479
And then they, like,
plug you in.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:10.115
-They've infused the room
so I can, like, feel it deeply.

00:04:10.115 --> 00:04:12.285
We do so many, like,
walk-throughs,

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:14.186
where we have, like,
multiple spaces

00:04:14.186 --> 00:04:17.456
and the sensor plugged in and
they're warm and they're going

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:19.892
and we all are kind of a part
of this process

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.027
together of creating the names.

00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:24.097
I love that you are
really interested

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:25.464
in the creation of the name.

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:28.601
So much more goes behind it.
-I'm very impressed by it.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.035
And congratulations on that.

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:31.537
Congratulations on the book.
-Thank you.

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:33.205
-And congratulations on --
I'm sure it's going to be

00:04:33.205 --> 00:04:35.474
the best Christmas card
of anyone I know.

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:37.510
-I'm gonna send you one.
-You better. You better.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.345
We'll send you one.
Hilary Duff, everyone.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:42.014
Her new book,
"My Little Sweet Boy,"

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:43.282
is on sale everywhere now,

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:44.450
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.618
And stick around.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.954 line:0%
We'll be right back with
Please Don't Destroy.

